WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.600
PostgreSQL.

2
00:00:01.900 --> 00:00:07.318
It is the default serious open-source database, and quietly, an AI platform too.

3
00:00:07.318 --> 00:00:08.267
Let's see why.

4
00:00:09.467 --> 00:00:14.300
PostgreSQL is a relational database that stretches in three useful directions.

5
00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:22.300
Together they mean you should ask a question before reaching for a dedicated vector database: does Postgres already do the job?

6
00:00:23.500 --> 00:00:25.533
Here are the three extensions that matter.

7
00:00:25.833 --> 00:00:31.467
JSONB stores documents as binary JSON that you can index and query inside.

8
00:00:31.767 --> 00:00:35.000
Built-in full-text search gives ranked keyword retrieval.

9
00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:41.200
And pgvector adds an embedding column type, so cosine similarity becomes an ORDER BY.

10
00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:46.167
Because it is all one system, a single statement can do everything.

11
00:00:46.467 --> 00:00:50.533
First, filter rows using fields inside the JSON metadata.

12
00:00:50.833 --> 00:00:54.633
Then order what is left by vector distance to the query embedding.

13
00:00:54.933 --> 00:00:56.633
And return the top five chunks.

14
00:00:57.833 --> 00:00:59.867
Here is a minimal version in code.

15
00:01:00.167 --> 00:01:06.467
Each chunk is one row, holding its JSON metadata and a fifteen hundred thirty six number embedding.

16
00:01:06.767 --> 00:01:09.900
The WHERE clause filters on a field inside the JSON.

17
00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:14.433
Then the arrow operator orders by cosine distance, and we take five.

18
00:01:15.633 --> 00:01:18.900
Here is a real story from a mission control RAG system.

19
00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:24.533
Version one used Postgres for metadata and a separate vector database for embeddings.

20
00:01:24.833 --> 00:01:29.033
Sync bugs left ghosts: deleted reports whose embeddings lived on.

21
00:01:29.333 --> 00:01:31.208
Version two is one Postgres.

22
00:01:31.208 --> 00:01:37.633
The chunk, its metadata, and its embedding are the same row, so a delete is a delete everywhere.

23
00:01:37.933 --> 00:01:39.967
Fewer moving parts, and one backup.

24
00:01:41.167 --> 00:01:41.954
Let's recap.

25
00:01:41.954 --> 00:01:45.433
Postgres is the default serious open-source database.

26
00:01:45.733 --> 00:01:49.500
JSONB, full-text search, and pgvector extend it.

27
00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:52.200
One database can serve a whole RAG stack.

28
00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:56.132
So ask first whether Postgres already does the job.

29
00:01:56.132 --> 00:01:57.200
Next up: MySQL.
